  "answer_text": "It is legally and practically **impossible to buy a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel** (*Pteromys momonga*) as a pet. \n\nIf you have seen websites or online listings claiming they can ship a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel to you, **it is a scam.** \n\n---\n\n### Why You Cannot Buy a Japanese Dwarf Flying Squirrel\n\n1. **Strict Japanese Export Laws:** The Japanese dwarf flying squirrel is endemic to Japan (specifically the islands of Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu). Japan has incredibly strict conservation laws and wildlife protection acts that prohibit the capture, commercial sale, and export of its native species. \n2. **No Domestic Breeders:** Because they cannot be legally exported, there are no legitimate captive-breeding populations of *Pteromys momonga* anywhere else in the world (such as the U.S., UK, or Europe). \n3. **Severe Legality Issues:** Attempting to smuggle one across borders is a serious international crime. In many regions, owning or importing non-native exotic wildlife is strictly banned.\n\n### The Internet \"Cute Photo\" Misconception\nMany of the incredibly cute, round, \"cotton ball\" photos that go viral online labeled as the \"Japanese dwarf flying squirrel\" are actually:\n* **The Siberian Flying Squirrel** (*Pteromys volans*), specifically the *Ezo momonga* subspecies found on the northern Japanese island of Hokkaido. They are also protected and not available as pets.\n* **Handmade Art Dolls:** Many viral images are actually hyper-realistic, poseable plushies made of polymer clay and faux fur by independent artists. \n\n---\n\n### What You Can Get Instead\n\nIf you love the look and concept of these animals, you have two main options:\n\n#### 1. Real, Legal Pet Alternatives\nIf you are looking for a small, gliding pocket pet that is legal and bred in captivity, you can consider:\n* **The Southern Flying Squirrel (*Glaucomys volans*):** This species is native to North America. Legitimate, USDA-licensed breeders raise them in captivity. They look very similar to Japanese flying squirrels and can bond closely with humans. *(Note: Owning them is illegal in 26 U.S. states, and others require permits, so you must check your local state laws.)*\n* **Sugar Gliders (*Petaurus breviceps*):** While they are marsupials rather than rodents, sugar gliders are highly popular, widely available, and legal in many more jurisdictions. They require specialized care, a specific diet, and must be kept in pairs. \n\n#### 2. Realistic Plushies & Art Dolls\nBecause the real animal cannot be kept as a pet, many fans buy high-quality replicas:\n* **Wild Republic** makes a popular and highly realistic *Cuddlekins Eco Japanese Dwarf Flying Squirrel* plush toy.\n* **Etsy or Tedsby** artists frequently sell beautifully crafted, life-sized, poseable handmade replica dolls of the Japanese dwarf flying squirrel.",
